WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.302 --> 00:00:01.390
(dramatic music)

00:00:01.390 --> 00:00:04.256
- [Narrator] This is a test
launch of an Iranian rocket.

00:00:04.256 --> 00:00:06.320
(speaking in foreign language)
(rocket whooshing)

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:07.950
Iran has said the rocket would be used

00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:09.880
to carry satellites into space

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:11.480
but some Western countries have said

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:14.290
that Iran could potentially
use rockets like this

00:00:14.290 --> 00:00:16.080
to carry a nuclear device.

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:19.090
- We are looking to prevent Iran

00:00:19.090 --> 00:00:20.587
from acquiring a nuclear weapon.

00:00:20.587 --> 00:00:23.590
(speaking in foreign language)

00:00:23.590 --> 00:00:25.100
- [Narrator] As Iran
reached new milestones

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:26.260
in uranium enrichment

00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:29.260
and limited access to inspectors in 2021,

00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:31.850
questions have intensified
over what is actually happening

00:00:31.850 --> 00:00:33.350
at some nuclear facilities

00:00:33.350 --> 00:00:35.640
where it handles uranium and plutonium,

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:37.790
the key ingredients for a nuclear bomb.

00:00:37.790 --> 00:00:40.140
- These gaps in monitoring will start

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:42.670
to raise questions
about Iran's intentions.

00:00:42.670 --> 00:00:44.260
- [Narrator] Iran has repeatedly denied

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:45.510
that it is making a bomb

00:00:45.510 --> 00:00:48.110
and said sites like these produce energy,

00:00:48.110 --> 00:00:50.975
medical equipment and scientific research.

00:00:50.975 --> 00:00:54.892
(speaking in foreign language)

00:00:58.383 --> 00:00:59.910
- [Narrator] But nuclear
experts says this hardware

00:00:59.910 --> 00:01:02.060
can potentially be used to produce weapons

00:01:02.060 --> 00:01:04.810
and note the country has been
using its nuclear capabilities

00:01:04.810 --> 00:01:06.920
as leverage with the
international community.

00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:08.891
- Almost all of the technologies

00:01:08.891 --> 00:01:11.760
have a civilian nuclear energy component

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:14.060
but they also have a
nuclear weapons component.

00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:15.300
- [Narrator] So what do we actually know

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:17.310
about Iran's nuclear infrastructure?

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:19.773
And how it might be used
to make nuclear weapons?

00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:22.790
Iran has been building its nuclear program

00:01:22.790 --> 00:01:24.040
for half a century.

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:26.030
- In the 1060s and 1970s

00:01:26.030 --> 00:01:28.534
when Iran was ruled by the Shah,

00:01:28.534 --> 00:01:31.170
there was an initial interest
in nuclear technology,

00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:33.460
spurred on by the United States

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:36.090
and actually some of Iran's
oldest nuclear facilities

00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:37.960
were built in that period.

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:39.440
- [Narrator] Jeffrey Lewis teaches classes

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:40.440
on arms control

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:42.780
and is a nonproliferation advocate.

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:44.640
- After the Shah fell,

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:46.430
all of that cooperation was cut off

00:01:46.430 --> 00:01:49.630
and Iran had a kind of
fitful relationship trying

00:01:49.630 --> 00:01:51.690
to revive that nuclear program on and off

00:01:51.690 --> 00:01:53.843
through the 1980s and 1900s

00:01:53.843 --> 00:01:56.020
until we entered the early 2000s

00:01:56.020 --> 00:01:58.440
when Iran made a very
significant commitment

00:01:58.440 --> 00:01:59.580
to a nuclear program.

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:00.670
- [Narrator] In the early 2000s,

00:02:00.670 --> 00:02:02.780
satellite imagery began to reveal sites

00:02:02.780 --> 00:02:05.730
like Natanz where Iran
says it enriches uranium

00:02:05.730 --> 00:02:07.300
for its nuclear power plants.

00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:10.550
- Natanz is a complex
where Iran conducts most

00:02:10.550 --> 00:02:13.180
of its uranium enrichment activities.

00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:14.780
- [Narrator] Kelsey
Davenport is an analyst

00:02:14.780 --> 00:02:16.900
at a nonpartisan organization

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:18.420
that promotes arms control.

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:20.340
- This facility is quite large.

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:24.380
It's big enough to house
about 100,000 centrifuges.

00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:25.780
- [Narrator] Centrifuges are devices

00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:29.420
that spin uranium oxide to
extract the useful isotopes,

00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:30.320
in other words.

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:33.340
- You want to remove the vast majority

00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:35.007
of uranium that is useless

00:02:35.007 --> 00:02:38.800
from a nuclear reaction perspective.

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:43.320
And really just leave a condensed quantity

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:44.500
of the good stuff.

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:46.890
- [Narrator] As part of
a 2015 nuclear agreement,

00:02:46.890 --> 00:02:48.710
Tehran had agreed to allow inspectors

00:02:48.710 --> 00:02:50.660
to monitor it nuclear facilities,

00:02:50.660 --> 00:02:53.820
limit the quantity of its
uranium to 300 kilograms

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:55.760
and limit enrichment to a key level

00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:58.440
of no more than 3.67%.

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:00.840
But after the United States
withdrew from the deal

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:03.440
and reimposed sanctions in 2018,

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:05.690
Iran began breaching that limit.

00:03:05.690 --> 00:03:08.300
This year, Iran announced
it would limit inspections

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:09.980
to sites like Natanz

00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:13.640
where it's been enriching uranium to 4.5%.

00:03:13.640 --> 00:03:15.670
- At 5%, you have reactor fuel.

00:03:15.670 --> 00:03:18.630
At 20%, you have scary reactor fuel.

00:03:18.630 --> 00:03:21.470
And at 90%, you have
the material for a bomb.

00:03:21.470 --> 00:03:22.870
- [Narrator] And in February of this year,

00:03:22.870 --> 00:03:24.940
Tehran said it could dramatically increase

00:03:24.940 --> 00:03:26.860
its levels of enrichment.

00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:30.777
(speaking in foreign language)

00:03:40.666 --> 00:03:42.690
- If you enrich to say 5%,

00:03:42.690 --> 00:03:46.065
it sounds like you haven't
gone very far of the way to 90%

00:03:46.065 --> 00:03:48.740
for a bomb but to go from zero to 5%

00:03:48.740 --> 00:03:51.090
is basically half the work

00:03:51.090 --> 00:03:52.610
that it will take to go to 90%.

00:03:52.610 --> 00:03:54.500
So if you have an enrichment capability,

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:56.410
you really are going to have the capacity

00:03:56.410 --> 00:03:58.200
to enrich to the levels

00:03:58.200 --> 00:03:59.810
that would be needed for a nuclear weapon.

00:03:59.810 --> 00:04:00.643
- [Narrator] And there's one site

00:04:00.643 --> 00:04:04.040
where Iran has already taken
that enrichment a step further.

00:04:04.040 --> 00:04:04.980
Fordow.

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:06.460
It was constructed in secret

00:04:06.460 --> 00:04:10.140
and only discovered by
intelligence agencies in 2009.

00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:12.820
- Fordow is much smaller,

00:04:12.820 --> 00:04:14.630
much more consistent with the size

00:04:14.630 --> 00:04:16.960
of a nuclear weapons facility.

00:04:16.960 --> 00:04:19.311
And its enrichment halls are placed

00:04:19.311 --> 00:04:20.680
under a mountain

00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:23.570
where they would be extremely
difficult to attack.

00:04:23.570 --> 00:04:25.560
And so when Fordow was revealed,

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:27.980
I think most of us looked
at the characteristics

00:04:27.980 --> 00:04:29.358
of that facility and thought

00:04:29.358 --> 00:04:31.540
that really looks more like something

00:04:31.540 --> 00:04:33.250
that you would build
if you wanted a weapon

00:04:33.250 --> 00:04:35.530
than it does if you were
planning to make fuel

00:04:35.530 --> 00:04:36.580
for a power plant.

00:04:36.580 --> 00:04:38.340
- [Narrator] Iran halted
production at Fordow

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:40.470
under the 2015 nuclear deal

00:04:40.470 --> 00:04:45.180
but in 2021, it said it resumed
uranium enrichment to 20%.

00:04:45.180 --> 00:04:46.850
It says it's enriching to this level

00:04:46.850 --> 00:04:49.240
for one of its research reactors in Tehran

00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:50.670
and that the facility's underground due

00:04:50.670 --> 00:04:53.400
to a risk of US or Israeli attack.

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:55.240
- All of these factors suggest

00:04:55.240 --> 00:04:57.934
that the Fordow facility
was likely being designed

00:04:57.934 --> 00:05:00.350
to produce this material

00:05:00.350 --> 00:05:03.580
for a weapons program if
Iran made the decision

00:05:03.580 --> 00:05:04.940
to pursue the bomb.

00:05:04.940 --> 00:05:07.120
- [Narrator] Another key step
in manufacturing the weapon

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:11.153
is the ability to convert
enriched uranium into a metal.

00:05:11.153 --> 00:05:14.890
And at Isfahan, Iran has
a uranium metal facility.

00:05:14.890 --> 00:05:16.980
- And this is quite concerning

00:05:16.980 --> 00:05:19.520
because uranium metal is another

00:05:19.520 --> 00:05:21.900
of these dual use areas.

00:05:21.900 --> 00:05:24.790
Iran claims that it's
pursuing this technology

00:05:24.790 --> 00:05:27.070
to produce uranium metal fuel

00:05:27.070 --> 00:05:28.760
for its research reactor.

00:05:28.760 --> 00:05:30.420
- [Narrator] So far, inspectors say Iran

00:05:30.420 --> 00:05:34.080
has only constructed 3.6
grams of uranium metal,

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:36.220
far less than the tens of kilos needed

00:05:36.220 --> 00:05:39.380
for a bomb and it's a
purity below weapons grade

00:05:39.380 --> 00:05:42.410
but still in breach of the
2015 nuclear agreement,

00:05:42.410 --> 00:05:44.930
which prohibited the production
of the metal all together.

00:05:44.930 --> 00:05:46.460
- I would say the facility as a whole

00:05:46.460 --> 00:05:48.550
is fundamentally civilian.

00:05:48.550 --> 00:05:50.959
The act of making uranium metal

00:05:50.959 --> 00:05:55.670
is one of those 50/50

00:05:55.670 --> 00:05:58.150
I would prefer you not
do it kind of things.

00:05:58.150 --> 00:06:00.330
- [Narrator] Iran has
several other facilities,

00:06:00.330 --> 00:06:04.300
including Iraq where it could
potentially produce plutonium.

00:06:04.300 --> 00:06:07.890
In early 2021, analysts estimated
it could have taken Iran

00:06:07.890 --> 00:06:11.160
between nine months and two
years to build a nuclear device

00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:13.220
but some analysts say limiting inspections

00:06:13.220 --> 00:06:15.760
may shorten that timeframe.

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:16.593
- I don't think we're going

00:06:16.593 --> 00:06:20.700
to see Iran dash for a bomb anytime soon

00:06:20.700 --> 00:06:22.670
but in this limbo,

00:06:22.670 --> 00:06:24.020
Iran will certainly seek

00:06:24.020 --> 00:06:27.670
to preserve the nuclear technologies

00:06:27.670 --> 00:06:29.430
and materials necessary

00:06:29.430 --> 00:06:32.580
to pursue a bomb down the
road if it chooses to do so.

00:06:32.580 --> 00:06:33.814
- [Narrator] Iran has issued a number

00:06:33.814 --> 00:06:36.040
of statements that have
been interpreted as a threat

00:06:36.040 --> 00:06:37.553
by Western countries.

00:06:37.553 --> 00:06:41.470
(speaking in foreign language)

00:06:56.240 --> 00:06:57.754
- [Narrator] Analysts say Iran's rhetoric,

00:06:57.754 --> 00:07:00.520
breaches of the 2015 nuclear deal

00:07:00.520 --> 00:07:03.110
and rocket launches may actually be aimed

00:07:03.110 --> 00:07:05.180
at one main goal, to be at the top

00:07:05.180 --> 00:07:06.860
of the US president's agenda

00:07:06.860 --> 00:07:08.830
and negotiate the lifting of sanctions

00:07:08.830 --> 00:07:10.250
as international pressure

00:07:10.250 --> 00:07:12.260
and the pandemic have thrown the country

00:07:12.260 --> 00:07:13.950
into a deep economic crisis.

00:07:13.950 --> 00:07:17.150
- It's been very transparent and upfront

00:07:17.150 --> 00:07:20.550
about what measures it intends to violate

00:07:20.550 --> 00:07:23.560
and it has informed the
International Atomic Energy Agency

00:07:23.560 --> 00:07:25.900
of those violations in advance

00:07:25.900 --> 00:07:28.130
so that they can be monitored.

00:07:28.130 --> 00:07:31.400
That tells us that these
measures are about leverage

00:07:31.400 --> 00:07:33.200
and about creating pressure

00:07:33.200 --> 00:07:36.690
and are not indicative of a
dash towards nuclear weapons.

00:07:36.690 --> 00:07:39.440
(dramatic music)

